WEBVTT

00:00.000 --> 00:10.000
The United States continues to fly high in the Olympic Games, and the Royal Family sees

00:10.000 --> 00:15.540
the 15-year-old U.S. sensation set a world and Olympic record in the 100-meter butterfly.

00:15.540 --> 00:20.720
She is Sharon Stooder of Glendora, California, and this is the way the Perk Miss picks up

00:20.720 --> 00:23.720
her third Olympic medal amid hearty plaudits from the crowd.

00:23.720 --> 00:29.360
She beats out the previous record holder, Ada Polk of the Netherlands, while Cathy Ellis

00:29.360 --> 00:31.160
of the U.S. is third.

00:31.160 --> 00:35.880
Two days later, Sharon won her fourth Olympic medal.

00:35.880 --> 00:39.480
The United States scores a stunning upset in the women's platform diving.

00:39.480 --> 00:42.680
Here is Ingrid Kramer Engel of Germany, the heavy favorite.

00:42.680 --> 00:47.520
However, she is not good enough against Leslie Bush of the U.S., who noses out the champion

00:47.520 --> 00:54.120
by almost a point and a half.

00:54.120 --> 00:59.200
In third place, behind the American and German, is Galina Alexieva, and the Soviet flag is

00:59.200 --> 01:03.920
raised beside the other two.

01:03.920 --> 01:06.880
Then the action moves to the stadium for the track events.

01:06.880 --> 01:11.280
First action is the men's 100-meter, and in the stands is the immortal sprinter, Jesse

01:11.280 --> 01:12.320
Owens.

01:12.320 --> 01:15.780
Heavy favorite in the dash is Bob Hayes in the far lane.

01:15.780 --> 01:20.520
The wind was too brisk in the semifinal, and a world record Hayes set was disallowed.

01:20.520 --> 01:26.320
Hayes had the race in his pocket the minute he left the starting blocks, and slow motion

01:26.320 --> 01:29.240
reveals the stride of the 190-pound sprinter.

01:29.240 --> 01:44.040
He has more power than Grace, but as anyone can see, it brings home the gold medal.

01:44.040 --> 01:49.040
No wind in this heat, Hayes does the 110-seconds plant, and once more is the Stars and Stripes

01:49.040 --> 01:55.400
to hail another U.S. gold medal.

01:55.400 --> 02:00.000
Naomi Atayas of the U.S. set an Olympic mark in the semifinals of the 100-meter dash, and

02:00.000 --> 02:05.120
now in the finals, she runs her rivals into the ground to reach the tape in 11 and 4 tenths

02:05.120 --> 02:06.120
seconds.

02:06.120 --> 02:10.720
She upsets her teammate, Edith McGuire, by two yards as she flies across.

02:10.720 --> 02:25.840
Yes, the American Eagle flies high in Tokyo.

